    {
     "chapter": "THE PITCH",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "WeWork was founded in twenty ten, in New York, by Adam Neumann and Miguel McKelvey.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "2010",
          "gold",
          230
         ],
         [
          "NEW YORK",
          "white",
          140
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The idea was simple. Lease big office floors, split them into desks and small rooms, and rent them out by the month.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flow",
        "a": "BIG FLOORS",
        "a_sub": "leased for years",
        "b": "SMALL DESKS",
        "b_sub": "rented monthly",
        "caption": "THE MODEL"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Free coffee, beer on tap, glass walls, and a promise: this isn't an office. It's a community.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "ww_space",
        "wiki": [
         "WeWork office",
         "WeWork coworking space",
         "WeWork"
        ],
        "lower": "A WeWork space",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "NOT AN OFFICE",
           "white",
           130
          ],
          [
           "A COMMUNITY",
           "gold",
           150
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "But there was a catch. WeWork signed leases that lasted many years. Its members could leave with a month's notice.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "THE MISMATCH",
        "items": [
         [
          "YEARS",
          "WEWORK'S LEASES",
          "red"
         ],
         [
          "1 MONTH",
          "MEMBERS' NOTICE",
          "white"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 160,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "If the economy slowed down, the customers could walk away. The rent bills would stay.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "CUSTOMERS LEAVE",
          "white",
          120
         ],
         [
          "RENT STAYS",
          "red",
          170
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Neumann didn't sell WeWork as a real estate company. He sold it as a tech company.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "neumann",
        "wiki": [
         "Adam Neumann",
         "Adam Neumann WeWork"
        ],
        "lower": "Adam Neumann, WeWork co-founder",
        "dim": 0.4,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "NOT REAL ESTATE",
           "white",
           120
          ],
          [
           "TECH",
           "gold",
           230
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And that matters, because investors pay a lot more for tech companies.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "TECH",
          "gold",
          200
         ],
         [
          "= HIGHER PRICE",
          "white",
          130
         ]
        ]
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE $47 BILLION",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "In twenty seventeen, the Japanese giant SoftBank and its CEO, Masayoshi Son, started pouring billions into WeWork.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "2017",
          "gold",
          220
         ],
         [
          "SOFTBANK",
          "white",
          160
         ],
         [
          "BILLIONS IN",
          "muted",
          100
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "By January twenty nineteen, WeWork was valued at forty seven billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 47,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": "B",
        "over": "JANUARY 2019",
        "label": "ON PAPER",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 240
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The company was growing fast. In twenty eighteen, revenue hit about one point eight billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 1.8,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": "B",
        "over": "2018",
        "label": "REVENUE",
        "color": "gold",
        "size": 230
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "But that same year, it lost about one point six billion.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_s1",
        "url": "https://www.fool.com/investing/2019/08/14/wework-files-updated-ipo-massive-losses.aspx",
        "source": "The Motley Fool · Aug 14, 2019",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "compare",
         "title": "WEWORK · 2018",
         "items": [
          [
           "$1.8B",
           "REVENUE",
           "gold"
          ],
          [
           "-$1.6B",
           "NET LOSS",
           "red"
          ]
         ],
         "size": 160,
         "stagger": 1.2,
         "vs": false
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "For every dollar it brought in, it lost almost ninety cents.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "EVERY $1 IN",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "~90¢ LOST",
          "red",
          170
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And its future lease payments added up to around eighteen billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "value": 17.9,
        "prefix": "$",
        "suffix": "B",
        "over": "LEASE OBLIGATIONS",
        "label": "PER THE IPO FILING",
        "color": "red",
        "size": 220
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Still, the plan was to go public, and cash in on that forty seven billion.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "NEXT STOP",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "WALL STREET",
          "gold",
          160
         ]
        ]
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE IPO",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "In August twenty nineteen, WeWork filed its paperwork to go public. And investors finally got to read the details.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "date",
        "month": "AUGUST",
        "day": "14",
        "year": "2019",
        "tag": "THE IPO FILING"
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Neumann's special shares had twenty votes each. Public investors would get one.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "VOTES PER SHARE",
        "items": [
         [
          "20",
          "NEUMANN'S SHARES",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "1",
          "PUBLIC INVESTORS",
          "white"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 200,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The filing also showed that WeWork had paid Neumann five point nine million dollars for the trademark to the word, We.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_we",
        "url": "https://foxbusiness.com/business-leaders/wework-ceo-adam-neumann-returns-trademark-money",
        "source": "Fox Business · 2019",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "stamp",
         "org": "TRADEMARK DEAL",
         "title": "THE WORD \"WE\"",
         "stamp": "$5.9M",
         "stamp_at": 0.9
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "After the backlash, he gave the money back.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THE $5.9M",
          "white",
          150
         ],
         [
          "RETURNED",
          "gold",
          170
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And it showed that WeWork leased space in buildings that Neumann had a stake in.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THE LANDLORD",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "WAS THE CEO",
          "red",
          150
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Investors did the math. Reports said the expected valuation dropped to around ten billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "THE VALUATION",
        "items": [
         [
          "$47B",
          "JANUARY 2019",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "~$10B",
          "SEPTEMBER 2019",
          "red"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 160,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": false
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "On September twenty fourth, twenty nineteen, Neumann stepped down as CEO.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "WEWORK · SEP 24, 2019",
        "title": "ADAM NEUMANN, CEO",
        "stamp": "OUT",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Six days later, the IPO was officially pulled.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "WEWORK · SEP 30, 2019",
        "title": "THE IPO",
        "stamp": "WITHDRAWN",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "WHO PAID",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "In October, SoftBank took control of WeWork, now valued at around eight billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "NINE MONTHS LATER",
        "items": [
         [
          "$47B",
          "JANUARY",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "~$8B",
          "OCTOBER",
          "red"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 170,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": false
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And the rescue came with a deal for Neumann, reported at up to one point seven billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_payout",
        "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2019/10/22/softbank-reportedly-ends-wework-ownership-debacle-with-a-1-7-billion-payout-to-adam-neumann",
        "source": "TechCrunch · Oct 22, 2019",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "counter",
         "value": 1.7,
         "prefix": "$",
         "suffix": "B",
         "over": "THE EXIT DEAL",
         "label": "AS REPORTED",
         "color": "gold",
         "size": 230
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "That included nearly a billion dollars to buy his shares, a five hundred million dollar credit line, and a hundred and eighty five million in consulting fees.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "title": "THE DEAL",
        "items": [
         "~$1B FOR HIS SHARES",
         "$500M CREDIT LINE",
         "$185M CONSULTING"
        ],
        "size": 85,
        "last_gold": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Weeks later, WeWork announced thousands of layoffs.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "video",
        "media": "office_empty",
        "search": "empty office desks",
        "search_en": "empty office desks",
        "start": 0.0,
        "dim": 0.4,
        "lower": "Layoffs",
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "THOUSANDS",
           "white",
           160
          ],
          [
           "LAID OFF",
           "red",
           170
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Here's the hidden cost.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "THE HIDDEN\nCOST",
        "tint": "red",
        "size": 150
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The founder walked away a billionaire. Thousands of employees walked away without a job. And many of them held stock that was now worth a fraction of what they had been told.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "WHO PAID",
        "items": [
         [
          "BILLIONAIRE",
          "THE FOUNDER",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "NO JOB",
          "THOUSANDS OF EMPLOYEES",
          "red"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 140,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": true
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "chapter": "THE FALL",
     "sentences": [
      {
       "text": "Then, in twenty twenty, the pandemic emptied offices around the world.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "video",
        "media": "empty_city",
        "search": "empty city office building",
        "search_en": "empty city office building",
        "start": 0.0,
        "dim": 0.4,
        "lower": "2020",
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "2020",
           "red",
           220
          ],
          [
           "EMPTY OFFICES",
           "white",
           130
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In October twenty twenty one, WeWork finally went public, through a merger with a blank check company, at a value of about nine billion dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_spac",
        "url": "https://rebusinessonline.com/bowx-acquisition-corp-agree-to-buy-wework-plans-to-take-company-public-at-9b-valuation/",
        "source": "REBusiness · 2021",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "counter",
         "value": 9,
         "prefix": "$",
         "suffix": "B",
         "over": "OCTOBER 2021",
         "label": "FINALLY PUBLIC",
         "color": "gold",
         "size": 230
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "It never turned a profit.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "PROFIT",
          "white",
          170
         ],
         [
          "NEVER",
          "red",
          220
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "In November twenty twenty three, WeWork filed for bankruptcy.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "stamp",
        "org": "WEWORK · NOV 2023",
        "title": "CHAPTER 11",
        "stamp": "FILED",
        "stamp_at": 0.9
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "By the end of that year, the company once valued at forty seven billion was worth less than a hundred million on the stock market.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "WEWORK",
        "items": [
         [
          "$47B",
          "2019",
          "gold"
         ],
         [
          "<$100M",
          "END OF 2023",
          "red"
         ]
        ],
        "size": 160,
        "stagger": 1.2,
        "vs": false
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "It came out of bankruptcy in June twenty twenty four. Smaller, with a new majority owner, the software company Yardi Systems.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_exit",
        "url": "https://www.newser.com/story/351625/wework-emerges-from-bankruptcy.html",
        "source": "Newser · Jun 13, 2024",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "bigtext",
         "stagger": 0.25,
         "lines": [
          [
           "JUNE 2024",
           "gold",
           170
          ],
          [
           "NEW OWNER",
           "white",
           140
          ]
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "It cut more than four billion dollars in debt and closed about a hundred and seventy locations.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "title": "THE RESTART",
        "items": [
         "$4B+ DEBT CUT",
         "~170 LOCATIONS CLOSED"
        ],
        "size": 90,
        "last_gold": true
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "And Adam Neumann? In twenty twenty two, the venture firm Andreessen Horowitz backed his new real estate startup, Flow, with three hundred and fifty million dollars.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "print",
        "media": "print_flow",
        "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2022/08/15/a16z-andreessen-horowitz-backs-adam-neumann-wework-new-venture-flow-real-estate/",
        "source": "TechCrunch · Aug 15, 2022",
        "rotate": -1.0,
        "fallback": {
         "type": "counter",
         "value": 350,
         "prefix": "$",
         "suffix": "M",
         "over": "FLOW · 2022",
         "label": "NEUMANN'S NEXT STARTUP",
         "color": "gold",
         "size": 220
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "According to TechCrunch, it was the firm's biggest single check ever.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "bigtext",
        "stagger": 0.25,
        "lines": [
         [
          "THEIR BIGGEST",
          "white",
          140
         ],
         [
          "CHECK EVER",
          "gold",
          170
         ]
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "The hidden lesson? A great story can be worth more than a business. Until someone reads the numbers.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "flash",
        "text": "READ THE\nNUMBERS",
        "tint": "red",
        "size": 150
       }
      },
      {
       "text": "Which company do you think is the next WeWork? Tell me in the comments, and follow Hidden Cost for a new case every week.",
       "scene": {
        "type": "endcard",
        "sub": "THE WEWORK CASE"
       }
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
